<h2 id="proof">Dynamic generation proof</h2>

The honest proof level for this run is **dynamic evidence-driven lane assembly**, not a fully generated workflow machine. That is still useful, but it is not the same claim.

<PatternCards items={generationProof} label="Dynamic generation proof" />

What the receipts prove:

| Receipt | Value |
| --- | ---: |
| Event records | 410 |
| Typesense machine discovery events | 1 |
| Typesense scout queries | 176 |
| Typesense scout results | 176 |
| Local JoelClaw result events | 44 |
| Hydrated JoelClaw session events | 9 |
| Proposed items | 9 |
| Assembled workflow lanes | 9 |

The lane set is task-specific to `recent-system-memory-kernel-improvements`: the workflow plan contains nine item ids from this run, five classifications, three lane patterns, and review targets derived from the selected system scope. The run also discovered four Typesense machines before scouting.

<h3 id="proof-gap">What this does not prove</h3>

This run does not prove that a real Pi planner generated an XState machine from scratch. The current hunt script still contains static ingredients: correction phrases, system/project theme libraries, and a static phase list. The runtime then uses the focus, discovered machines, retrieved hits, hydration results, and confidence/decision rules to assemble candidate lanes.

That means the right label is:

```txt
dynamic evidence-driven lane assembly over a static theme/pattern library
```

The wrong label would be:

```txt
fully generated XState workflow machine
```

<h3 id="proof-requirements">Required proof for the next report</h3>

The next dynamic-workflow proof needs these pinned artifacts before execution:

1. Planner prompt and transcript receipt from a real Pi or other real agent lane.
2. Generated `workflow.xstate-machine.v1` config artifact.
3. Dynamic plan artifact binding step ids to the generated machine.
4. Verification contract.
5. Hashes for the machine config, plan, verification contract, and any emitted source or harness artifacts.
6. Verifier result that checks those hashes and confirms the run executed the generated machine config, not a static fallback.
7. D2 figure generated from the pinned machine artifact, not hand-authored from prose.

## Run coverage

The dream crossed recent and mid-term memory: April 20 through June 9, 2026. It found `pi`, `claude`, and `codex` sessions across `blaine`, `dark-wizard`, `flagg`, and `panda`, then hydrated nine JoelClaw session receipts behind the local boundary.

| Dimension | Count |
| --- | ---: |
| Candidate items | 9 |
| Pending candidates | 7 |
| Needs more evidence | 2 |
| Typesense chunk receipts | 66 |
| Hydrated JoelClaw session receipts | 9 |
| Total locator receipts | 75 |
| Candidate graph nodes | 10 |
| Candidate graph edges | 9 |

<h2 id="access">Access adapter shape</h2>

Cloudflare should talk to memory through a leased port, not through the raw database. The worker asks for one memory operation envelope. A trusted JoelClaw relay or tailnet runner evaluates policy, executes the search/hydration, redacts evidence, and returns signed receipt locators.

```ts
type DreamMemoryLease = {
  actorId: string;
  scope: "system" | "project" | "support";
  purpose: string;
  operations: Array<
    | "search-sessions"
    | "hydrate-session"
    | "export-redacted-evidence"
    | "write-dream-artifact"
  >;
  sourceFilters: {
    machines?: string[];
    runtimes?: Array<"pi" | "claude" | "codex">;
    since?: string;
  };
  queryBudget: number;
  expiresAt: string;
  policyVersion: string;
  idempotencyKey: string;
};
```

The adapter returns distilled candidates, graph edges, and receipt locators. It does not return raw session transcripts to public review pages, generated prompts, sandbox logs, or long-lived Cloudflare environment variables.

## HITL report node

Treat this template as the seed of a package export, not as one prototype directory:

```ts
type WzrrdHitlReportNode = {
  package: "@joelhooks/wzrrd-hitl-report";
  export: "renderReviewSurface";
  template: "joel/tufte-mdsvx";
  input: {
    runId: string;
    artifactRefs: string[];
    reviewKind: "dream" | "support" | "workflow" | "capability";
    redactionPolicy: "public-redacted" | "private-tailnet" | "operator-only";
  };
  output: {
    buildDir: string;
    surfaceHash: string;
    summaryRef: string;
  };
};
```

In workflow terms, this node belongs after synthesis/verification and before external delivery:

```txt
pin artifacts
  -> synthesize candidate review data
  -> invoke installed HITL report node
  -> write review-surface artifact
  -> request wzrrd.site.publish lease
  -> publish expiring Wzrrd page
  -> wait for human accept/edit/reject
  -> route to apply, rollback, or needs-more-evidence
```

That gives us a clean ports-and-adapters split:

| Boundary | Owns |
| --- | --- |
| package export | SvelteKit/MDSvX report rendering contract |
| workflow node | when review is needed, which template to invoke, and where output artifacts land |
| review gate | accept, edit, reject, or request more evidence |
| capability lease | permission to publish externally |
| Wzrrd adapter | actual Wzrrd API call and publish receipt |

<h2 id="template">Report standard</h2>

This report was rewritten from the hand-rolled HTML review into a SvelteKit/MDSvX report initialized from `joel/tufte-mdsvx@0.1.0`, the local Wzrrd Tufte notes template. The same source pattern is already proven in `prototypes/shitrat-brain-proposal-ui`.

Going forward, project reports should use this pattern unless they are intentionally throwaway local scratch:

1. Create or clone a SvelteKit/MDSvX report source.
2. Keep prose in `.svx`, reusable display primitives in `src/lib`, and generated static output in `build`.
3. Use margin notes for receipts and caveats, stat lines for run facts, tables/cards for review decisions, and code blocks only for contracts worth reusing.
4. Publish the built static directory with Wzrrd, defaulting to `noindex` and explicit expiry for review pages.
5. Capture durable decisions in `.brain/`; do not treat the published page as the long-term memory.

## What did not happen

No support inbox sweep ran. No candidate was accepted. No Brain, prompt, skill, kernel, or package surface was auto-patched from the dream output.

